      "mastery": 0.39970421723435146,
      "prior_failures": 0,
      "text": "The gradient of the tangent at a point is what the gradients of the secants approach as the second point slides in towards the first. A secant taken at any finite separation is still a secant: its gradient is close to the tangent's, not equal to it. For y = x^2 the limit works out to 2x.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 681
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "remediate",
      "level": "targeted",
      "targets": "tangent_as_secant",
      "mastery": 0.39970421723435146,
      "prior_failures": 0,
      "text": "The calculation should represent the limit of the secant gradients as the distance between the two points approaches zero rather than using a fixed interval. To find the gradient, you must set up the difference quotient (f(x) - f(a)) / (x - a) and evaluate it as x approaches 4.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 685
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "To find the gradient of a line, how many points on that line do you usually need to know?",
      "opening": true,
      "version": 687
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "In this context, a point is just a location on a curve, like (x, y) coordinates. The gradient is the measure of how steep that curve is at any given spot.\n\nIf we wanted to find the steepness of a straight line, how many points would we need to know?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 689
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "Actually, to calculate the gradient (the steepness) of a straight line, we usually need at least two points. If you have two points, say (x1, y1) and (x2, y2), how do you think you would calculate the change in height over the change in distance between them?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 691
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "socratic",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "Exactly! You'd look at the difference in the vertical values and divide it by the difference in the horizontal values. \n\nIf we have two points, (x1, y1) and (x2, y2), how would you write that calculation as a formula?",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 693
    },
    {
      "item_id": "",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "explain",
      "level": "summary",
      "targets": null,
      "mastery": 0.0,
      "prior_failures": 1,
      "text": "The gradient of a curve at a single point. A gradient normally needs two points, so at first this looks like it is asking for something impossible.\n\nTake a second point nearby and you get a secant, whose gradient you can work out the ordinary way. Slide that second point in towards the first and the secant gradients settle towards a number. No finite separation gives it exactly \u2014 a secant is always a secant \u2014 but the value they approach is perfectly well defined, and that limit is what 'the gradient at a point' means.\n\nThe gradient of the tangent at a point is what the gradients of the secants approach as the second point slides in towards the first. A secant taken at any finite separation is still a secant: its gradient is close to the tangent's, not equal to it. For y = x^2 the limit works out to 2x.\n\nAn example \u2014 not your question, and the numbers are different:\nThe tangent to y = x^3 at x = 1.\nSecants from x = 1 to x = 1 + h have gradient ((1 + h)^3 - 1)/h,\nwhich multiplies out to 3 + 3h + h^2.\nAs h shrinks towards 0 that approaches 3.\nThe tangent's gradient is 3.",
      "opening": false,
      "version": 694
    },
    {
      "item_id": "ca_tan_p1",
      "concept_id": "limit_concept",
      "move": "present",
      "level": "formula",
      "targets": null,
